visualization_scene = ExtResource("2_bn84h")
concept_text = "Binary Search is a BLAZING FAST search — but it only works on SORTED arrays. It repeatedly cuts the search range in HALF by comparing the target to the MIDDLE element.

How it works on [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13], target = 7:

Step 1 — left = 0, right = 6. Mid = (0+6)//2 = 3. arr[3] = 7. EQUAL! Found at index 3!

Done in ONE step.

How it works on [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13], target = 6:

Step 1 — left = 0, right = 6. Mid = 3. arr[3] = 7. 7 > 6, so target is in the LEFT half. New right = 2.

Step 2 — left = 0, right = 2. Mid = 1. arr[1] = 3. 3 < 6, so target is in the RIGHT half. New left = 2.

Step 3 — left = 2, right = 2. Mid = 2. arr[2] = 5. 5 < 6, so target is in the right. New left = 3.

Step 4 — left = 3, right = 2. left > right. NOT FOUND.

Four steps. Not bad.

The magic: every comparison CUTS the search space in half. For 1 million items, you need only about 20 steps (because log₂(1,000,000) ≈ 20). Compare to linear search, which might need 1 million steps.

Performance:
- Time: O(log n) — EXTREMELY fast
- Space: O(1) — no extra memory
- ONLY works on SORTED data — if the data isn't sorted, you MUST sort it first
- Best case: O(1) — target is the middle element
- Average and worst case: O(log n)
- Used in: dictionaries, phone books, sorted databases, git bisect, basically anywhere you search sorted data"

real_world_example = "Imagine you're looking up a word in a DICTIONARY. You don't start at page 1 and flip every page. Instead:

1. Open the dictionary roughly in the MIDDLE.
2. See what letter is on that page (say, \"M\").
3. If your word starts with \"M\" or later, ignore the LEFT half. If earlier, ignore the RIGHT half.
4. Repeat with the remaining half — open it in the middle, see the letter, ignore half, repeat.

Within 5-10 \"openings\" of the dictionary, you can find any word, even in a 1000-page dictionary. That's binary search.

The dictionary is SORTED alphabetically — that's the key. If the dictionary were random, you'd have to flip every page (linear search).

Another example: looking up a name in a phone book (back when those existed). Same approach — open in the middle, compare the first letter, ignore half, repeat.

A third example: Git's \"bisect\" command. It finds which commit introduced a bug by binary searching through the commit history. You mark a \"good\" commit and a \"bad\" commit, and git checks out the middle commit. You tell it whether that commit is good or bad, and it cuts the range in half. After about log₂(N) steps, it finds the exact commit.

A fourth example: looking up a word in a sorted word list for autocomplete. Your phone's autocomplete uses binary search to find the first word that starts with your prefix, then shows the next few words.

Why is binary search so fast? Because each step ELIMINATES half the remaining data. For 1 million items, you need only ~20 steps. For 1 billion items, you need only ~30 steps. This is incredibly efficient.

The trade-off: your data MUST be sorted first. If you sort once and then search many times, the sort cost is worth it. If you only search once, linear search might be faster overall."

guided_example = "Let's search for 23 in [2, 5, 8, 12, 16, 23, 38, 56], step by step.

Step 1 — Set the pointers.

    left = 0        # index of first element
    right = 7       # index of last element

We're searching the entire array: indices 0 through 7.

Step 2 — Calculate the middle.

    mid = (0 + 7) // 2 = 3
    arr[3] = 12

The middle is index 3, which holds 12.

Step 3 — Compare.

    Is 12 < 23? YES → target is in the RIGHT half.
    New left = mid + 1 = 4.

We ignore everything from index 0 to 3. Only indices 4-7 remain.

Step 4 — New middle.

    left = 4, right = 7
    mid = (4 + 7) // 2 = 5
    arr[5] = 23

Step 5 — Compare.

    Is 23 == 23? YES! FOUND at index 5!

We found it in just 2 steps. With linear search, it would have taken 6 steps (checks 2, 5, 8, 12, 16, 23).

What if the target is NOT in the array? Say we search for 99:

Step 1 — left=0, right=7, mid=3, arr[3]=12. 12 < 99 → left = 4.
Step 2 — left=4, right=7, mid=5, arr[5]=23. 23 < 99 → left = 6.
Step 3 — left=6, right=7, mid=6, arr[6]=38. 38 < 99 → left = 7.
Step 4 — left=7, right=7, mid=7, arr[7]=56. 56 < 99 → left = 8.
Step 5 — left=8, right=7. left > right. STOP. Not found.

Five steps. Even for \"not found\", we did way fewer checks than a linear search would have (which would have done all 8)."

recap_text = "- Binary Search works ONLY on sorted arrays
- Repeatedly cuts the search range in HALF
- Compare target to the MIDDLE element:
  - If equal: found!
  - If smaller: target is in the LEFT half (move right pointer)
  - If larger: target is in the RIGHT half (move left pointer)
- Time: O(log n) — extremely fast (1 billion items in ~30 steps)
- Space: O(1) — no extra memory
- Best case: O(1) — target is the middle
- Worst case: O(log n) — target is at one end or not in list
- REQUIRES sorted data — sort first if needed
- Used in: dictionaries, sorted databases, git bisect, autocomplete, search engines
- The classic example of \"divide and conquer\" search
- If you can't sort the data, you must use Linear Search instead"

challenge_instruction = "Drag the blocks to perform Binary Search on a sorted array. We're searching for 7 in [1, 3, 5, 7, 9]."
challenge_blocks = Array[String](["arr = [1, 3, 5, 7, 9]", "target = 7", "left = 0", "right = 4", "while left <= right:", "    mid = (left + right) // 2", "    if arr[mid] == target: print(mid); left = right + 1", "    elif arr[mid] < target: left = mid + 1", "    else: right = mid - 1"])
